{
  "requirement": "`habeas author <full-name> [--config <name>] [--stdout] [-v|--verbose]` MUST assess one\nexisting requirement or named example in the current project. It MUST report the\nselected subject, backend, model, evidence destination, and usage-record path on\ncompletion. The evidence destination MUST identify the evidence-file path by\ndefault or stdout when `--stdout` is selected. In stdout mode, the completion\nreport and diagnostics MUST go to stderr. `author-evidence` MUST remain an alias\nfor the single-subject command.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "## Judgment: `requirements.authoring.invocation` is satisfied\n\nThe implementation is complete and marked, and the tests cover the points that matter. The two weaker spots are noted at the end and don't change the result.\n\n### Implementation\n\n- **Command syntax and alias** (`src/main.rs`, `enum Command`, `Author` variant):\n  - The variant takes a positional `subject`, `--config <name>`, `--stdout` and `-v/--verbose`.\n  - `#[command(alias = \"author-evidence\")]` makes the alias the same command.\n  - An `ArgGroup` requires either a subject or `--all`, so the two conflict.\n  - `--all` conflicts with `--stdout`.\n  - The `{impl}` marker is on the variant, and `main` marks the `Author` match arm.\n- **Assess one existing requirement or named example**:\n  - `AssessmentSources::prepare` (`src/authoring/inputs.rs`) loads the project's definitions.\n  - It rejects any name that is missing or is a term, with the error `no matching requirement or named example`.\n  - The same code accepts both requirements and examples.\n  - The entry points `author_evidence`, `author_evidence_with_output`, `configured_assessment` and `configured_record` in `src/authoring/mod.rs` carry out the assessment. Each has an `{impl}` marker.\n- **Reporting on completion** (`main`, single-subject branch):\n  - It prints `subject: backend / model`, then `Evidence: <path>` and `Usage: <path>`.\n  - `AuthoringOutcome` carries the subject, backend, model, optional `evidence_path` and `usage_path`.\n  - In file mode the evidence path is the actual `evidence/<subject>.json` path.\n  - In stdout mode `evidence_path` is `None`, so the destination is reported as `Evidence: stdout`.\n- **Stderr in stdout mode**:\n  - In stdout mode `main` writes the whole completion report with `eprintln!`.\n  - The only thing written to stdout is the evidence JSON, via `configured_assessment`.\n  - Errors are returned from `main` and go to stderr.\n  - The false-judgment line also goes to stderr.\n- **Behavior outside this requirement**: the usage, persistence, reporting and read-denial behavior in these functions is marked as implementing other requirements, so it is not unmarked extra behavior.\n\n### Tests (`tests/authoring_cli.rs`)\n\n- **`stdout_emits_only_evidence_json_without_changing_project_files`**:\n  - It covers true, false and FIXME judgments, with and without existing evidence, and with and without `--verbose`.\n  - It checks that stdout parses as pure evidence JSON and that project files are unchanged.\n  - It checks that stderr contains `SUBJECT: ollama / MODEL`, `Evidence: stdout` and `Usage:`, and that the usage file exists.\n  - It also exercises the `author-evidence` alias.\n- **`ollama_cli_delivers_sources_records_fixme_and_keeps_usage_content_free`** runs the alias in file mode. It checks that stdout carries the model plus `Evidence:` and `Usage:`, and that the evidence file is written.\n- **`false_is_a_completed_assessment`** exercises `--config mock` and checks that the false line goes to stderr only.\n- **`verbose_single_author_reports_only_false_rationale_without_source_inputs`** covers `-v` and `--verbose` for false and true judgments.\n- **`author_rejects_unknown_subjects_and_conflicting_targets`** covers an unknown subject, the conflicting-argument cases, and the alias with `--all`. It also checks that evidence stays untouched.\n- **Failure paths** such as untracked reads, invalid submissions and state errors confirm stdout is empty and diagnostics go to stderr in `--stdout` mode.\n- **Named examples**: `initial_prompt_substitutes_subject_and_mandatory_inputs_into_the_template` runs `author requirements.ready_case` and confirms an example is accepted and assessed. It is marked `initial_prompt` rather than `invocation`, but it does exercise the behavior.\n\n### Minor observations (not gaps)\n\n- The file-mode tests assert only that `Evidence:` appears in stdout, not the exact path.\n- `AuthoringOutcome`, `AuthoringSubject`, `EvidenceOutput` and `AssessmentKind` are cite-only. They are plain data declarations with no behavior of their own, and the behavior that uses them is marked `{impl}`.",
  "references": "ccca9dd3881a15262a67117460d9658cbc0518cda94fb2fb15ad5597f0371fe8",
